That single failure is what exposed the defect below.

=== DEFECT FOUND: T01.2.4 PARTIAL RETRACTION WAS NEVER IMPLEMENTED ===
Cascade invalidated EVERY forward dependent, including objects that still
retained a valid upstream reference. That contradicts two ratified sources:

  N-9 "What It Binds": "T01.2.4 partial retraction: an object retaining at
  least one valid upstream reference is re-versioned, not invalidated."

  N-9 "Consequences Accepted": "A dependent supported by ten Facts, one of
  which is invalidated, is handled by the partial-retraction rule rather than
  by cascade -- the boundary between the two must be maintained carefully."

  IOM 3.2 (Fact transitions): ACTIVE -> INVALIDATED on "All attesting
  Evidence retracted". Not "some".

Demonstrated before the fix: a Fact derived from two Evidence objects was
INVALIDATED when only ONE was retracted.

=== FIX APPLIED (oip/cascade.py only) ===
- _retains_valid_upstream(stored, doomed): an upstream still attests unless it
  is RETRACTED/INVALIDATED, or is itself doomed by this same cascade.
- A `doomed` set seeded with the origin and extended as dependents become
  eligible. Necessary because eligibility is computed before any mutation for
  rollback safety, so an upstream reading ACTIVE may already be condemned.
  Breadth-first ordering guarantees an upstream is decided before its child.
- CascadeResult.partially_retracted / .partial_count report the spared
  objects, so the N-9 boundary is visible rather than silent.
- Cascade still performs NO interpretation: it does not re-version. Producing
  a new version with reduced support is the owning engine's act; cascade
  "alters status only, never content" (N-9).

Verified: ACTIVE <=> retains >=1 non-withdrawn upstream, in every case.
Single-parent chains still cascade fully (Evidence -> Fact -> Problem ->
Pattern all INVALIDATED). Retracting ALL upstream still invalidates.
T01.2.4's three acceptance criteria now pass, and the rule is type-agnostic
(verified on Fact-from-Evidence and Problem-from-Facts).

